# type_index and type_db_versions are separate locks: a regeneration can report stamps that do not describe the loaded index

## Steps to Reproduce

1. TODO: Add steps

## Expected Behavior

TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Disclosed by the B039 lane 2026-08-30 as a deliberate decision, not found in
review. Filed so the decision is tracked rather than rediscovered.

## Defect

`Backend` holds two independent locks:

    src/server/mod.rs:124   type_index:       Arc<RwLock<Option<Arc<TypeIndex>>>>
    src/server/mod.rs:133   type_db_versions: Arc<RwLock<Option<TypeDbVersions>>>

They are written non-atomically (`src/server/mod.rs:1236` and `:1247`), and
every reader takes one lock at a time. So a reader can observe a NEW index
paired with STALE stamps, and two concurrent regenerations can interleave to
leave the pair permanently describing different loads.

## Severity: low, and be honest about why

The consequence is a wrong REPORT, not wrong analysis — diagnostics still come
from whatever index is loaded. It needs concurrent `openplanet.regenerateTypeDb`
invocations, which are user-triggered from the palette, so it takes deliberate
double-firing to hit.

But it is worth fixing eventually because the report is the entire user-visible
point of B039's change. `before`/`after`/`changed` exists so a user can tell
whether regenerating actually did anything. A field that can silently describe
a different load than the one in memory undermines the one thing it is for.

## Work

Hold the stamps and the index under a SINGLE lock so they cannot disagree —
e.g. one `RwLock<Option<LoadedTypeDb>>` where `LoadedTypeDb { index, versions }`.

Note the cost honestly: `type_index` is read in roughly ten places
(`mod.rs` :770, :837, :956, :1518 and others), so this is a mechanical refactor
of every read site, not a two-line change. That is why the B039 lane correctly
declined to do it inline.

Do NOT 'fix' this by acquiring both write locks in a fixed order. That
serialises writers but readers still take one lock at a time, so a reader can
still observe a torn pair. Only a single lock actually closes it.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)

Red: drive two concurrent `regenerateTypeDb` executions against a server whose
DB changes between them, and assert the reported `after` stamps equal the
stamps of the index actually loaded. Deterministic reproduction will likely
need an injected pause between the two writes.

Variants:
- concurrent regenerations -> reported stamps always describe the loaded index
- a reader mid-regeneration never sees index and stamps from different loads
- single-threaded behaviour completely unchanged (the B039 tests still pass)
- a FAILED regeneration leaves index and stamps consistent with each other

## Related

B039 (introduced `type_db_versions`; the lane disclosed this rather than
shipping it silently), GH #59 (naming the loaded build in the log).

## Correction to the analysis above (coordinator, 2026-08-30)

The B039 lane found the fact that removes `just add a mutex` as an apparent
option, and it changes the ticket.

**This is not purely a concurrency bug.** `TypeIndex` does not retain the
stamps it parsed, which is why `after` has to be a SECOND read of the same
files rather than a value carried out of the load. So the pair can tear on a
SINGLE THREAD: if the dump is rewritten between the load and the probe, the
stamps describe a different database than the one in memory, with no
concurrency involved at all.

Consequences:

- Serialising the two writes fixes nothing, and neither does one lock over
  both, if the stamps are still obtained by re-reading the file afterwards.
  Both would produce a confident 'fixed' commit that leaves the defect.
- The real fix is to make `TypeIndex::load` RETURN the stamps it parsed —
  the load already reads those bytes — so the stamps are a product of the same
  read that produced the index, and then to store index and stamps together.
  The single lock is necessary but not sufficient; sourcing the stamps from
  the load is the part that actually closes it.

The original `do not fix by ordering the two write locks` warning still
stands, and this is the stronger form of the same point: every lock-shaped fix
addresses the visible symptom rather than the mechanism.

## Correction to the correction (coordinator, 2026-08-30)

My previous append overstated the fix, having inherited the overstatement from
the B039 lane's comment. Codex caught it and the lane confirmed. **B064 is TWO
defects, not one, and they need two different fixes.**

**(a) Shared-state interleaving.** `type_index` and `type_db_versions` are
separate locks written non-atomically, so a reader can observe a new index with
stale stamps. Fix: collapse both into one lock
(`RwLock<Option<LoadedTypeDb>>`). This is the mechanical ~10-read-site change.

**(b) Load/probe TOCTOU.** `TypeIndex::load` parses the files, then
`TypeDbVersions::probe` reads them AGAIN. If the dump changes in between, the
stamps describe a different database — on a single thread, with no concurrency
and no lock involved. Fix: have `TypeIndex::load` return the stamps it already
parsed (it reads those bytes anyway), or otherwise feed both from ONE read.

I previously wrote that one lock is 'necessary but not sufficient' and that
sourcing stamps from the load 'is the part that actually closes it'. That was
closer but still wrong in framing: these are not two parts of one fix, they are
two independent defects. (a) is a data race; (b) is a filesystem TOCTOU.
Fixing either leaves the other. A change that only collapses the locks will
look complete and close nothing on the single-threaded path.

The original warning stands unchanged: do NOT fix (a) by ordering the two write
locks, because readers still take one at a time.

## Lane decision (coordinator, 2026-08-30)

**Own lane (`lane-b064`), parallel with everything else.** Sole owner of
`src/server/mod.rs`; no other live lane touches it. lane-b057 is in
`completion.rs`/`hover.rs`/`index.rs`, lane-b060-b062 is in `checker.rs`/
`unused.rs`/`manifest.rs`, lane-b039-b041 is in the colour provider.

Branch from master tip including this claim.

**Do both defects or neither is closed.** The two corrections above are the
whole ticket: (a) the two locks collapse into one, (b) `TypeIndex::load`
returns the stamps it already parsed so the probe is not a second read.
A commit that does only (a) will look complete, pass a concurrency test, and
leave the single-threaded TOCTOU untouched. Report which of the two each test
you write would catch — if any test would pass with only one half applied,
that test does not pin what it claims to.